-- title:
--   Points on the line of P₀ lie in ℤP₀
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a field with decidable equality, let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $F$ which is elliptic (its discriminant is a unit), and let $\ell$ be a natural number. Let $x_0,y_0,x,y \in F$ be such that $(x_0,y_0)$ and $(x,y)$ are nonsingular points of the affine model of $W$, witnessed by $h_0$ and $h$, and suppose that [`ModularCurve.InLine W ℓ x₀ x`](def/ModularCurve_KatzLevelP.html#L21) holds, i.e. there is a natural number $a$ with $1 \le a$ and $a \le (\ell - 1)/2$ (natural subtraction and division) such that $x \cdot \Psi_a^2(x_0) = \Phi_a(x_0)$, where $\Phi_a$ and $\Psi_a^2$ are the division polynomials `W.Φ` and `W.ΨSq` of $W$ in the $x$-coordinate. The conclusion is that the affine point `WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.some x y h` of the group of points of $W$ lies in the subgroup `AddSubgroup.zmultiples` generated by `WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.some x₀ y₀ h₀`, that is, $(x,y)$ is an integer multiple of $(x_0,y_0)$.
--
--   This is the statement that a point whose $x$-coordinate is pinned by the division-polynomial relation $x\Psi_a^2(x_0) = \Phi_a(x_0)$, i.e. $x = x([a]P_0)$, lies in the cyclic subgroup generated by $P_0$ (indeed it equals $\pm[a]P_0$). It is used in the construction of diamond operators on full-level modular curve data, where an `InLine` condition on a $\Gamma_1$-type datum must be converted into membership in $\mathbb{Z}P_0$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_InLine_some_mem_zmultiples_some_of_nonsingular.lean

